  "id": "DrugBank_DB01003 - MONDO_grouped_11786_5324_24332",
  "verbalization": "Cromoglicic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Allergic Rhinitis through a series of interconnected indications. It is indicated for Vasomotor Rhinitis, a condition characterized by nasal symptoms triggered by factors other than allergens, and it is also indicated for Allergic Asthma, which can lead to secondary Rhinitis. The evidence suggests that Cromoglicic Acid may act as a bronchodilator and anti-inflammatory agent, potentially modulating inflammatory pathways that contribute to nasal symptoms. The indirect pathway through Mizolastine and Montelukast further supports a role in allergic inflammation, as both are second-generation antihistamines and leukotriene receptor antagonists. While the direct link between Cromoglicic Acid and Allergic Rhinitis is not explicitly stated, the cumulative evidence implies a potential mechanism involving inflammatory regulation, possibly through the modulation of mast cell degranulation and airway inflammation. This integrated pathway highlights the importance of considering multiple indications and their interconnections in understanding the therapeutic potential of Cromoglicic Acid in allergic rhinitis.",
